"Internal Error ()\nciteturn41348view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn41347view0\",\"id\":38}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://capra.cs.cornell.edu/latte26/paper/latte26-final1.pdf: (400) Timeout fetching\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGroupDPO: Memory-Efficient Group-Wise Direct Preference Optimization (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.15602)\nciteturn41348view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 23; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn41347view1\",\"id\":38}); Total lines: 1504\nL0@P0: GroupDPO: Memory-Efficient Group-Wise Direct Preference Optimization\nL1@P0: Jixuan Leng*\nL2@P0: Carnegie Mellon University\nL3@P0: jixuanl@cs.cmu.edu\nL4@P0: Si Si\nL5@P0: Google DeepMind\nL6@P0: sisidaisy@google.com\nL7@P0: Hsiang-Fu Yu\nL8@P0: Google\nL9@P0: rofuyu@google.com\nL10@P0: Vinod Raman\nL11@P0: Google DeepMind\nL12@P0: vinodraman@google.com\nL13@P0: Inderjit S. Dhillon\nL14@P0: Google\nL15@P0: isd@google.com\nL16@P0: Abstract\nL17@P0: Preference optimization is widely used to align\nL18@P0: Large Language Models (LLMs) with prefer\u0002ence feedback. However, most existing meth\u0002ods train on a single positive-negative pair\nL19@P0: per prompt, discarding additional supervision\nL20@P0: available in preference datasets that typically\nL21@P0: contain multiple candidate responses. Moti\u0002vated by this limitation, recent work explores\nL22@P0: group-wise preference optimization, which\nL23@P0: jointly contrasts multiple responses for the\nL24@P0: same prompt, but its empirical behavior and\nL25@P0: scalability remain underexplored due to the\nL26@P0: memory overhead of group-coupled objectives.\nL27@P0: In this work, we present a unified empirical\nL28@P0: and systems study of group-wise preference\nL29@P0: optimization and develop a memory-efficient\nL30@P0: implementation for group-coupled objectives.\nL31@P0: By instantiating first-order linearization with\nL32@P0: objective-specific per-response coefficients, our\nL33@P0: implementation preserves first-order gradients\nL34@P0: while decoupling samples during backpropaga\u0002tion, substantially reducing peak memory us\u0002age and enabling scalable training with larger\nL35@P0: groups. Across offline and online settings, we\nL36@P0: show that leveraging multiple responses consis\u0002tently outperforms single-pair training. Further\u0002more, incorporating a negative log-likelihood\nL37@P0: (NLL) term on positive responses is critical for\nL38@P0: both performance gains and training stability.\nL39@P0: 1 Introduction\nL40@P0: Preference optimization has become a central com\u0002ponent of modern large language model (LLM)\nL41@P0: alignment. Rather than relying solely on supervised\nL42@P0: fine-tuning, many alignment pipelines train policy\nL43@P0: models using preference feedback that compares\nL44@P0: alternative responses to the same prompt. Early\nL45@P0: approaches relied on Reinforcement Learning from\nL46@P0: Human Feedback (RLHF) (Christiano et al., 2017;\nL47@P0: Ouyang et al., 2022; Yang et al., 2025b), where a re\u0002ward model is first trained then used to optimize the\nL48@P0: *Work done while interning at Google.\nL49@P0: policy model. More recently, Direct Preference Op\u0002timization (DPO) (Rafailov et al., 2023) and related\nL50@P0: methods (Meng et al., 2024; Hong et al., 2024; Liu\nL51@P0: et al., 2024b) simplify this pipeline by directly opti\u0002mizing the policy from preference data, bypassing\nL52@P0: reward-model training and policy-gradient RL.\nL53@P0: Despite these advances, most preference opti\u0002mization methods operate on pairwise comparisons\nL54@P0: between a single positive and negative response.\nL55@P0: In practice, however, preference datasets are of\u0002ten constructed by sampling multiple candidate re\u0002sponses for each prompt and evaluating them using\nL56@P0: human or automated feedback (Cui et al., 2023).\nL57@P0: To apply pairwise objectives such as DPO, these\nL58@P0: response sets are typically binarized by selecting\nL59@P0: a single positive and negative response while dis\u0002carding the remaining candidates. This reduction\nL60@P0: loses potentially useful supervision signals about\nL61@P0: the relative quality of responses within the group.\nL62@P0: As a result, recent work has begun exploring group\u0002wise or listwise preference learning methods that\nL63@P0: train on response sets rather than individual pairs.\nL64@P0: Group-wise preference optimization can provide\nL65@P0: richer supervision by jointly contrasting multiple\nL66@P0: candidate responses for the same prompt. Several\nL67@P0: recent methods extend pairwise preference learn\u0002ing to this setting using ranking or set-level objec\u0002tives (Song et al., 2024; Gupta et al., 2024; Liu\nL68@P0: et al., 2025b; Chen et al., 2024a). However, the\nL69@P0: practical behavior of these methods remains poorly\nL70@P0: understood, as prior work typically proposes indi\u0002vidual objectives and evaluates them in isolation,\nL71@P0: making systematic comparisons under consistent\nL72@P0: training settings difficult. Moreover, group-wise\nL73@P0: objectives introduce cross-response dependencies\nL74@P0: within each prompt group. Because the loss de\u0002pends on interactions between positive and nega\u0002tive responses, gradients become coupled across\nL75@P0: samples and require constructing a joint computa\u0002tion graph over the group. In naive implementa\u0002tions (von Werra et al., 2020; Gupta et al., 2024),\nL76@P0-1: arXiv:2604.15602v2 [cs.CL] 1 Sep 2026Figure 1: Overview of Group DPO and our memory-efficient surrogate implementation. Top: Standard DPO trains\nL77@P1: on a single positive-negative pair, while group-wise objectives leverage multiple responses but require joint forward\nL78@P1: and backward passes, leading to high memory usage and OOM with large group sizes. Bottom: Our surrogate avoids\nL79@P1: group-coupled backpropagation by precomputing per-sample coefficients in a no-gradient pass and optimizing a\nL80@P1: sample-level loss, substantially reducing peak memory overhead and enabling scalable training with larger groups.\nL81@P1: activations for all samples in a group must therefore\nL82@P1: be retained until backpropagation, causing activa\u0002tion memory to scale with group size and quickly\nL83@P1: become prohibitive. Consequently, memory costs\nL84@P1: restrict prior work to relatively small group sizes,\nL85@P1: limiting exploitation of group-level supervision.\nL86@P1: In this work, we study group-wise preference\nL87@P1: optimization from both practical and empirical per\u0002spectives. To address the computational challenges\nL88@P1: of group-wise objectives, we develop a memory\u0002efficient surrogate implementation that reformu\u0002lates the objective into a sample-level loss with\nL89@P1: matched first-order gradients. Our construction in\u0002stantiates a general first-order linearization princi\u0002ple for group-wise preference objectives: we derive\nL90@P1: objective-specific per-sample coefficients using a\nL91@P1: lightweight no-gradient forward pass and then per\u0002form standard token-level backpropagation with\nL92@P1: these coefficients. This decouples gradients across\nL93@P1: samples and eliminates the need to retain activa\u0002tions for samples simultaneously, making memory\nL94@P1: overhead largely independent of the group size.\nL95@P1: Using this implementation, we unify and com\u0002pare several representative group-wise objectives\nL96@P1: from prior work under controlled offline and on\u0002line alignment settings. Our results reveal two ro\u0002bust design principles. First, incorporating multi\u0002ple samples per prompt consistently outperforms\nL97@P1: single-pair training, indicating that group-level su\u0002pervision provides a richer learning signal. Second,\nL98@P1: group-wise training can be unstable: including an\nL99@P1: additional negative log-likelihood (NLL) term (Liu\nL100@P1: et al., 2024b; Pang et al., 2024; Wang et al., 2025;\nL101@P1: Grattafiori et al., 2024; Pal et al., 2024) over posi\u0002tive samples plays a crucial role in preventing train\u0002ing collapse and improving the final performance.\nL102@P1: We evaluate our approach across multiple mod\u0002els and training settings. Results on multi-domain\nL103@P1: benchmarks show that group-wise preference opti\u0002mization consistently outperforms single-pair train\u0002ing. Meanwhile, the proposed surrogate implemen\u0002tation significantly reduces memory usage while\nL104@P1: maintaining competitive training latency, making\nL105@P1: group-wise training more practical at larger scales.\nL106@P1: Our contributions are summarized as follows:\nL107@P1: • We unify representative group-wise objectives\nL108@P1: through a common coefficient view and provide\nL109@P1: a controlled offline and online comparison, show\u0002ing that the gains are robust across objectives.\nL110@P1: • We identify two transferable design principles:\nL111@P1: training with groups of responses improves over\nL112@P1: pairwise training, and positive-response NLL reg\u0002ularization stabilizes group-wise optimization.\nL113@P1: • We derive a sample-level surrogate for group\u0002wise losses with matching first-order gradients.\nL114@P1-2: • We show that this implementation reduces thepeak memory overhead and enables training with\nL115@P2: larger groups while maintaining good efficiency.\nL116@P2: 2 Related Work\nL117@P2: Preference Alignment. Aligning LLMs with hu\u0002man preferences has relied on RLHF (Christiano\nL118@P2: et al., 2017; Ouyang et al., 2022), where a reward\nL119@P2: model trained on preference comparisons is opti\u0002mized using policy gradient methods such as Prox\u0002imal Policy Optimization (PPO) (Schulman et al.,\nL120@P2: 2017). While effective, RLHF introduces computa\u0002tional overhead and training instability due to extra\nL121@P2: reward-model training and reinforcement learning.\nL122@P2: DPO (Rafailov et al., 2023) provides a simpler\nL123@P2: alternative by reformulating preference alignment\nL124@P2: as a classification problem over chosen and rejected\nL125@P2: responses, allowing the policy to be optimized di\u0002rectly without training an explicit reward model.\nL126@P2: Building on this formulation, subsequent work has\nL127@P2: explored extensions to improve training stability\nL128@P2: and robustness. One direction augments the ob\u0002jective with supervised learning signals to miti\u0002gate issues such as likelihood degradation and over\u0002optimization. For example, Regularized Preference\nL129@P2: Optimization (RPO) (Liu et al., 2024b) introduces\nL130@P2: a negative log-likelihood (NLL) term over chosen\nL131@P2: responses as an implicit regularizer, while Iterative\nL132@P2: Reasoning Preference Optimization (IRPO) (Pang\nL133@P2: et al., 2024) adopts a similar hybrid objective to\nL134@P2: stabilize online alignment for complex reasoning\nL135@P2: tasks. Another line of research investigates biases\nL136@P2: in preference learning, including length exploita\u0002tion and overconfidence, and proposes calibration\nL137@P2: and normalization methods to address them (Park\nL138@P2: et al., 2024; Liu et al., 2024a; Leng et al., 2025).\nL139@P2: Because DPO relies on a frozen reference model,\nL140@P2: several works have also explored reference-free\nL141@P2: formulations that simplify training. For exam\u0002ple, SimPO (Meng et al., 2024) removes the ref\u0002erence model and constructs the preference ob\u0002jective using length-normalized log-likelihoods of\nL142@P2: the policy, while ORPO (Hong et al., 2024) inte\u0002grates preference optimization directly into super\u0002vised fine-tuning through an odds-ratio objective.\nL143@P2: Beyond human-labeled comparisons, recent self\u0002improvement approaches such as SPIN (Chen et al.,\nL144@P2: 2024b) leverage iterative self-play to generate extra\nL145@P2: preference data for improvement during training.\nL146@P2: Group-wise Preference Learning. Although\nL147@P2: DPO operates on pairwise comparisons, modern\nL148@P2: preference datasets often contain multiple candi\u0002date responses for each prompt. Reducing these\nL149@P2: response sets to independent pairs discards use\u0002ful relative information and limits the supervision\nL150@P2: signal available during training. To address this\nL151@P2: limitation, recent work has increasingly explored\nL152@P2: group-wise or listwise preference learning, drawing\nL153@P2: connections to classical learning-to-rank methods.\nL154@P2: Several approaches extend preference optimiza\u0002tion beyond pairwise comparisons using ranking\u0002based objectives. Preference Ranking Optimization\nL155@P2: (PRO) (Song et al., 2024) and Listwise Preference\nL156@P2: Optimization (LiPO) (Liu et al., 2025b) general\u0002ize pairwise comparisons to full rankings using\nL157@P2: the Plackett-Luce model (Plackett, 1975). Multi\u0002Preference Optimization (MPO) (Gupta et al.,\nL158@P2: 2024) instead models preferences using a con\u0002trastive objective defined over the response groups.\nL159@P2: Similar group-wise or listwise learning paradigms\nL160@P2: have also been explored in recommendation sys\u0002tems, where methods such as S-DPO (Chen\nL161@P2: et al., 2024a), LPO4Rec (Li et al., 2025), and\nL162@P2: RankGR (Fu et al., 2026) leverage large candidate\nL163@P2: sets to improve ranking and retrieval performance.\nL164@P2: Despite these advances, prior work on group\u0002wise preference optimization has two key limita\u0002tions. First, algorithms are often proposed for dif\u0002ferent settings, lacking a unified comparison of\nL165@P2: group-wise objectives. Second, many methods op\u0002erate on relatively small response groups due to\nL166@P2: the memory overhead of group-wise losses. We\nL167@P2: address these limitations by systematically com\u0002paring representative methods under a unified ex\u0002perimental framework, studying larger group sizes,\nL168@P2: and introducing a memory-efficient surrogate im\u0002plementation whose GPU memory usage does not\nL169@P2: scale with group size, enabling scalable training.\nL170@P2: 3 Background\nL171@P2: Group DPO Setup. We consider a preference\nL172@P2: dataset where responses are organized into a collec\u0002tion G of groups. Each group g ∈ G corresponds\nL173@P2: to a prompt x and contains positive responses Pg\nL174@P2: and negative responses Ng. Responses within each\nL175@P2: group are treated as unordered, with no intra-set\nL176@P2: ranking. This setting arises naturally in practice:\nL177@P2: for example, in online training with rule-based out\u0002come rewards (e.g., math reasoning), responses are\nL178@P2: often partitioned only by correctness, and in rec\u0002ommendation systems feedback typically indicates\nL179@P2: preferred vs. non-preferred items without ordering.\nL180@P2-3: Implicit preference score. Following DPO, wedefine the implicit preference score as follows:\nL181@P3: uθ(y|x) = β\nL182@P3: \nL183@P3: log πθ(y|x) − log πref(y|x)\nL184@P3: \u0001\nL185@P3: ,\nL186@P3: a scaled log-likelihood ratio between the policy πθ\nL187@P3: and reference model πref, and β is a scaling factor.\nL188@P3: Unified Group DPO Objective View. A broad\nL189@P3: class of preference objectives can be written as\nL190@P3: Lgroup(θ) = 1\nL191@P3: |G|\nL192@P3: X\nL193@P3: g∈G\nL194@P3: ϕg\nL195@P3: \nL196@P3: uPg(θ), uNg(θ)\nL197@P3: \u0001\nL198@P3: (1)\nL199@P3: where uPg = {uθ(y|x) : y ∈ Pg} and similarly for\nL200@P3: uNg, and ϕg is method-specific, differing mainly\nL201@P3: in how scores are aggregated or contrasted within\nL202@P3: each group. Despite differences in loss definitions,\nL203@P3: these objectives share a common computational\nL204@P3: issue: cross-response coupling within each group,\nL205@P3: which makes direct optimization memory-intensive.\nL206@P3: To address this, we introduce a memory-efficient\nL207@P3: and gradient-equivalent surrogate loss in Section 4.\nL208@P3: 4 Memory-Efficient Surrogate\nL209@P3: Motivation: cross-response dependencies in\nL210@P3: group-wise objectives. Group-wise preference\nL211@P3: objectives couple responses within each prompt\nL212@P3: group. Many variants rely on cross-response inter\u0002actions (e.g., score difference between positive and\nL213@P3: negative responses up−un or softmax-based aggre\u0002gations such as log Pexp(un)), meaning that the\nL214@P3: gradient contribution of each response depends on\nL215@P3: the scores of other responses in the same group. As\nL216@P3: a result, the gradient for a single response cannot\nL217@P3: be computed from a purely local per-response loss.\nL218@P3: A straightforward implementation performs a\nL219@P3: joint forward pass over all responses in the group,\nL220@P3: computes the group-level loss, and backpropagates\nL221@P3: once through the resulting computation graph. This\nL222@P3: is analogous to the concatenated forward strategy\nL223@P3: used in single-pair DPO, where chosen and rejected\nL224@P3: responses are processed together. In the group-wise\nL225@P3: setting, however, this approach requires retaining\nL226@P3: activations for all responses. As a result, activation\nL227@P3: memory grows with the group size and becomes\nL228@P3: expensive for large group sizes or long sequences.\nL229@P3: Surrogate loss. To avoid constructing a joint\nL230@P3: computation graph over all responses, we formu\u0002late group-wise optimization as a sample-level sur\u0002rogate objective with matched first-order gradients.\nL231@P3: Let R =\nL232@P3: S\nL233@P3: g∈G(Pg ∪ Ng) denote the response\nL234@P3: set in the current batch. We first run a no-grad pass\nL235@P3: to obtain scores u˜i, and use them to compute per\u0002sample coefficients that quantify each response’s\nL236@P3: contribution to the original group objective. Fol\u0002lowing Eq. (1), we define coefficients ci\nL237@P3: , which are\nL238@P3: then treated as constants during backpropagation:\nL239@P3: ci =\nL240@P3: 1\nL241@P3: |G|\nL242@P3: ∂ϕg\nL243@P3: ∂ui\nL244@P3: \nL245@P3: \nL246@P3: \nL247@P3: \nL248@P3: \nL249@P3: \nL250@P3: \nL251@P3: \nL252@P3: u=˜u\nL253@P3: , i ∈ Pg ∪ Ng.\nL254@P3: We then optimize the surrogate objective\nL255@P3: Lsur(θ) = X\nL256@P3: i∈R\nL257@P3: ci ui(θ),\nL258@P3: which is first-order exact at the current parameter\nL259@P3: point: its gradient w.r.t. model parameters matches\nL260@P3: that of Lgroup. Algorithm 1 summarizes the coef\u0002ficient computation. Since the pass is no-grad, it\nL261@P3: does not retain any activations for backpropagation.\nL262@P3: Algorithm 1 Precompute Coeff for Group DPO\nL263@P3: Require: Group collection G with response parti\u0002tions (Pg, Ng); response set R =\nL264@P3: S\nL265@P3: g∈G(Pg ∪\nL266@P3: Ng); reference log-prob ri = log πref(yi\nL267@P3: | xi);\nL268@P3: scale β\nL269@P3: Ensure: Coeff {ci} for all i ∈ R\nL270@P3: 1: No-grad pass: p˜i ← log πθ(yi| xi), ∀i ∈ R\nL271@P3: 2: u˜i ← β(˜pi − ri), ∀i ∈ R\nL272@P3: 3: Initialize ci ← 0, ∀i ∈ R\nL273@P3: 4: for all g ∈ G do\nL274@P3: 5: for all i ∈ Pg ∪ Ng do\nL275@P3: 6: ci ← ci +\nL276@P3: 1\nL277@P3: |G|\nL278@P3: ∂ϕg(u)\nL279@P3: ∂ui\nL280@P3: \nL281@P3: \nL282@P3: \nL283@P3: \nL284@P3: \nL285@P3: \nL286@P3: \nL287@P3: \nL288@P3: u=˜u\nL289@P3: 7: end for\nL290@P3: 8: end for\nL291@P3: 9: return {ci}i∈R\nL292@P3: The choice of ϕg determines ci. Table 1 summa\u0002rizes the objectives and corresponding coefficients.\nL293@P3: Practical benefit. Because optimization oper\u0002ates on sample-level terms, backpropagation can\nL294@P3: be performed using standard token-level training\nL295@P3: primitives. As a result, peak activation memory\nL296@P3: becomes largely insensitive to group size (up to\nL297@P3: micro-batch effects; see Section 5.3 for more de\u0002tails), and sample-level techniques such as dynamic\nL298@P3: batch sizing and packing can be applied directly.\nL299@P3: 5 Experiments\nL300@P3: In this section, we conduct a series of ex\u0002periments using gemma3-4b-sft* (Team et al.,\nL301@P3: 2025), together with olmo-3-7b-it-sft and\nL302@P3: olmo-3.1-32b-it-sft (Olmo et al., 2025), in the\nL303@P3: *A SFT checkpoint from gemma-3-4b-pt and trained on\nL304@P3-4: allenai/Dolci-Instruct-SFT-No-Tools (Olmo et al., 2025).Table 1: Original per-group losses and derivatives. Define q(z) = σ(z) − 1. We report ∂ϕg/∂ui for i ∈ Pg.\nL305@P4: Method Original loss ϕg ∂ϕg/∂ui\nL306@P4: DPO − log σ\nL307@P4: \nL308@P4: ui − uj\nL309@P4: \u0001\nL310@P4: , i ∈ Pg, j ∈ Ng q\nL311@P4: \nL312@P4: ui − uj\nL313@P4: \u0001\nL314@P4: Margin − log σ\nL315@P4: \nL316@P4: u¯Pg − u¯Ng\nL317@P4: \u0001 q\nL318@P4: \nL319@P4: u¯Pg − u¯Ng\nL320@P4: \u0001\nL321@P4: |Pg|\nL322@P4: MPO − log\nL323@P4: \nL324@P4: \nL325@P4: X\nL326@P4: i∈Pg\nL327@P4: si\nL328@P4: \nL329@P4:  , sk =\nL330@P4: e\nL331@P4: uk\nL332@P4: P\nL333@P4: m∈Pg∪Ng\nL334@P4: e\nL335@P4: um\nL336@P4: si −\nL337@P4: si\nL338@P4: ρ\nL339@P4: , ρ =\nL340@P4: X\nL341@P4: i∈Pg\nL342@P4: si\nL343@P4: Softmax 1\nL344@P4: |Pg|\nL345@P4: X\nL346@P4: i∈Pg\nL347@P4: − log σ\nL348@P4: \u0010\nL349@P4: ui − log X\nL350@P4: j∈Ng\nL351@P4: e\nL352@P4: uj\nL353@P4: \u0011\nL354@P4: q(zi)\nL355@P4: |Pg|\nL356@P4: , zi = ui − log X\nL357@P4: j∈Ng\nL358@P4: e\nL359@P4: uj\nL360@P4: All-Pairs 1\nL361@P4: |Pg||Ng|\nL362@P4: X\nL363@P4: i∈Pg\nL364@P4: X\nL365@P4: j∈Ng\nL366@P4: − log σ\nL367@P4: \nL368@P4: ui − uj\nL369@P4: \u0001 1\nL370@P4: |Pg||Ng|\nL371@P4: X\nL372@P4: j∈Ng\nL373@P4: q\nL374@P4: \nL375@P4: ui − uj\nL376@P4: \u0001\nL377@P4: offline setting, and qwen3-4b-base (Yang et al.,\nL378@P4: 2025a) in the online setting, to address the follow\u0002ing questions: (1) Is Group DPO more beneficial\nL379@P4: than pairwise comparisons? (2) Is the negative log\u0002likelihood (NLL) loss term necessary in the group\u0002wise setting? (3) What are the practical memory\nL380@P4: and latency benefits of our surrogate implementa\u0002tion? Details of data preparation, evaluation, and\nL381@P4: hyperparameters are provided in Appendix A.\nL382@P4: Methods. We compare several group-wise pref\u0002erence objectives, including Margin, MPO, All\u0002Pairs, and Softmax (see Table 1 for their loss ex\u0002pressions), along with vanilla DPO as a baseline.\nL383@P4: • RFT. Rejection Fine-Tuning, which trains only\nL384@P4: on positive responses within each prompt group.\nL385@P4: • DPO (Rafailov et al., 2023). Direct Preference\nL386@P4: Optimization, which only trains on single pairs†.\nL387@P4: • Margin. A group-level variant optimizing mean\nL388@P4: reward gap between positive and negative groups.\nL389@P4: • MPO (Gupta et al., 2024). Multi-Preference Op\u0002timization maximizing probability over positives.\nL390@P4: • All-Pairs. A variant averaging the DPO loss over\nL391@P4: all positive-negative pairs in each group. Explic\u0002itly materializing these gives the pair-expansion\nL392@P4: implementation denoted as flatten in Section 5.3.\nL393@P4: • Softmax (Chen et al., 2024a). The loss contrasts\nL394@P4: one positive against softmax-aggregated nega\u0002tives; extended by aggregating over all positives.\nL395@P4: We implement all methods using our proposed sur\u0002rogate implementation and denote them with a “+”\nL396@P4: suffix in the tables. Importantly, the “+” suffix\nL397@P4: indicates a change in implementation rather than\nL398@P4: in the underlying objective: the corresponding “+”\nL399@P4: and vanilla variants use the same response groups\nL400@P4: and optimize the same objective, differing only in\nL401@P4: how backpropagation is executed. Appendix C ver\u0002ifies their gradient and multi-step update agreement\nL402@P4: †Unless otherwise specified, DPO in experiments uses\nL403@P4: randomly selected pairs.\nL404@P4: across objectives, optimizers, learning rates, and\nL405@P4: group sizes. The vanilla implementations of the\nL406@P4: group-wise objectives exceed GPU memory lim\u0002its under the main experimental setting, whereas\nL407@P4: the surrogate implementations enable training in\nL408@P4: this regime (see Section 5.3). Unless otherwise\nL409@P4: specified, all methods include an NLL loss term\nL410@P4: with coefficient 1.0, which is commonly used in\nL411@P4: preference optimization to prevent the log proba\u0002bility of positive responses from decreasing (Liu\nL412@P4: et al., 2024b; Pang et al., 2024; Wang et al., 2025;\nL413@P4: Grattafiori et al., 2024; Pal et al., 2024). We further\nL414@P4: apply a length-normalized loss to mitigate length\nL415@P4: bias (Olmo et al., 2025; Lambert et al., 2024; Meng\nL416@P4: et al., 2024; Yuan et al., 2023), as shown below:\nL417@P4: min\nL418@P4: πθ\nL419@P4: E(x,yp,yn)∼D\"\nL420@P4: − log σ\nL421@P4: \nL422@P4: β\nL423@P4: |yp|\nL424@P4: log πθ(yp | x)\nL425@P4: πref(yp | x)\nL426@P4: −\nL427@P4: β\nL428@P4: |yn|\nL429@P4: log πθ(yn | x)\nL430@P4: πref(yn | x)\nL431@P4: !\nL432@P4: − α\nL433@P4: 1\nL434@P4: |yp|\nL435@P4: log πθ(yp | x)\nL436@P4: #\nL437@P4: Offline Setting. We use prompts sourced from\nL438@P4: Dolci-Instruct-DPO (Olmo et al., 2025). For each\nL439@P4: prompt, candidate responses are sampled from mul\u0002tiple models and evaluated using a reward model.‡\nL440@P4: The top-k responses form the positive group, while\nL441@P4: the bottom-k form the negative group. Importantly,\nL442@P4: the reward scores are discarded after grouping,\nL443@P4: and responses within each group are treated as un\u0002ordered; that is, no intra-group ranking is assumed.\nL444@P4: For the 4B and 7B models, we use a group size of\nL445@P4: 16 (8 positive and 8 negative responses) and sweep\nL446@P4: β ∈ {2.0, 5.0, 10.0, 15.0} with a fixed learning\nL447@P4: rate of 1e−6. For the 32B model, the group size is\nL448@P4: ‡\nL449@P4-5: Skywork-Reward-V2-Qwen3-8B (Liu et al., 2025a).reduced to 8 and we fix β = 2.0 due to computa\u0002tional constraints. Additional training details and\nL450@P5: full results are provided in Appendices A and D.\nL451@P5: Online Setting. We use prompts sourced from\nL452@P5: DAPO-Math-17k (Yu et al., 2026). For each\nL453@P5: prompt, multiple responses are sampled from the\nL454@P5: policy model. A rule-based reward function is used\nL455@P5: to evaluate response accuracy, and the responses\nL456@P5: are partitioned into positive and negative groups\nL457@P5: based on correctness of the final answer. Unlike the\nL458@P5: offline setting, the resulting groups may be unbal\u0002anced; we only enforce the total group size, while\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Black Box to Toolbox: Human-Centered Explainability in Developer-Authored Agent Rules | Zenodo (https://zenodo.org/records/19700278)\nciteturn41348view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn41347view2\",\"id\":38}); Total lines: 165\n"
